\newlabel{fig:training}{{1}{3}{\textbf {Three-phase grokking arc} (mean~$\pm $~std, 5 seeds). Training accuracy reaches 100\% early; test accuracy stays near random chance ($\ln 97 \approx 4.6$ nats) until the abrupt grokking transition (mean step $\approx $2\,700). Test loss peaks \emph {above} the random-chance baseline during the plateau, confirming active suppression of generalisation before the algorithmic switch}{figure.caption.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:training@cref}{{[figure][1][]1}{[1][2][]3}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{fig:dissociation}{{2}{3}{\textbf {$\operatorname {tr}(H)$ collapses at grokking; SGLD-LLC does not} (mean~$\pm $~std, 5 seeds; both axes log scale). \emph {Left (blue):} $\operatorname {tr}(H)$ rises during memorisation then collapses $\sim \!4.1\times $—from $\sim \!52{,}000$ (plateau) to $\sim \!12{,}500$ (post-grokking). \emph {Right (purple):} SGLD-LLC stabilises at $\sim \!1{,}900$ and remains flat across the grokking transition. The two probes diverge because $\operatorname {tr}(H)$ captures local basin curvature (which changes) while SGLD-LLC captures global multiplicity (which does not)}{figure.caption.2}{}}
\newlabel{fig:dissociation@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][2][]3}{}{}{}}
